A||||13||james||oil in air cleaner
Z||000000||james||08-23-99||06:32 PM||||I own an 86 2M4 and i've noticed that my air filter is clogged with oil and that there is a small pool of oil in the air cleaner i was wondering what would cause this and how to fix it.
Z||000001||sluppy123||08-23-99||07:29 PM||||I've noticed the samething on my 85' 2M4. It's a small pool of oil just inside of the  air the inlet. It has to be coming up through the crankcase ventilation filter but I don't know why!!
Z||000002||james||08-23-99||07:40 PM||||i've narrowed it down to possibly a clogged PCV valve if that's all then it's a simple fix
Z||000003||Patrick||08-23-99||08:34 PM||||<br>This has been discussed here just recently. <p>If it is indeed a clogged PCV valve, consider yourself lucky.<p>The other possibility is an worn out engine.<IMG SRC="/ubb/frown.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p> 
Z||000004||batboy||08-24-99||06:05 AM||||It doesn't make any sense that oil is finding its way past the PCV if the valve is clogged. It's probably blow-by which means your pistons rings have a lot of wear.
Z||000005||theogre||08-24-99||09:45 AM||||you always get some blow by. even on a "perfect" motor. A cloged PCV can cause oil to blow up the breather.<p>replace the PCV and breather filter, then if it's still doing it more than a tiny bit start testing the rest.
Z||000006||overlord||08-24-99||07:22 PM||||woah...james, sounds like u have the same car as me..with the same problem..o ya...u got the same first name as me too..<p>too weird
Z||000007||Patrick||08-25-99||03:09 AM||||<br>batboy, you're a little confused about how the PCV system works.<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>The oil doesn't work it's way past the PCV valve into the air filter can. You're thinking of the wrong end of the system. The PCV is hooked up to manifold vacuum which in turn creates a vacuum in the crankcase. Air is drawn into the crankcase through the breather which is attached to the air filter can. That's how it's supposed to work.<p>When the PCV valve gets clogged, there is no longer a vacuum being created in the crankcase. A worn out engine makes this situation worse because pressure is added to the crankcase from "blow by" getting past the rings on the compression stroke. This built up air pressure has to go somewhere, so it <b>backs up</b> through the breather (not the PCV valve) into the air filter can. This crankcase air contains vaporized oil which then condenses and forms pools of oil within the air filter can.<p>I suppose this isn't as bad as having the main oil seals blowing out from the pressure.<p>batboy, I hope you don't mind this friendly correction. I just didn't want you possibly misleading the newbies.<IMG SRC="/ubb/biggrin.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p><br>
Z||000008||Smokey||08-25-99||03:05 PM||||I believe batboy was referring to the Crankcase separator patrick. But everyone mistakes that for a PCV valve. I even asked western auto for a PCV valve once and he laid that on the counter hehe
Z||000009||Patrick||08-25-99||04:54 PM||||<br>Well Smokey, if I may borrow a term from your post in another thread, now I'm the one who doesn't want to appear "ignorant".<p>What is a "Crankcase separator"? I've never heard of it.<p> 
Z||000010||batboy||08-25-99||07:17 PM||||Actually, I've never heard of a crankcase separator either. Pat, I'm never offended to be corrected when I'm wrong. Unfortunately, I'm even more confused now. I know that air in the crankcase contains vaporized oil, but if the PCV valve is completely blocked, how does this air "back up" into the air cleaner? Through the intake valves and intake manifold?
Z||000011||Patrick||08-25-99||08:12 PM||||<br>No, no, no, no, no.....<p>Hey batboy, I'm glad you're a good sport about this.<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>If the V8 in the your batmobile is set up anything like the V8 in my Z28, you should have a hose sticking into the valve cover on one bank (A) which is open to atmospheric pressure. This hose usually terminates inside of the air filter can in a modern vehicle. At the end of this hose inside of the air filter can will be some kind of filtering material. Older cars used to have a hollow type of cap which had a steel mesh type of material in it.<p>Sticking out of the valve cover on the other bank (B) should be the PCV valve with a hose attached. This hose goes to a connection usually at the base of the carburetor (or somewhere on the intake manifold)  which has access to manifold vacuum. <p>When the engine is running, manifold vacuum sucks air through the air filter can, through the filtering material, through the hose to bank (A), through the crankcase (where it picks up "blow by" gases), up through the PCV valve in bank (B), and then finally through the hose to the connection at the base of your carb which leads to the intake system and eventually combustion.<p>The whole idea of the PCV system is to burn harmful crankcase gases before they enter the atmosphere, as was the case with all older cars. The reason why "oil" backs up into the air filter can is because the connection between the can and the crankcase at bank (A) is passive, and when pressure builds up in the crankcase due to a clogged PCV valve, the gases (and oil vapour) simply follow the path of least resistance.<p>Does that make it any clearer, or have I totally confused everyone?<IMG SRC="/ubb/biggrin.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>By the way, it's possible that the "crankcase separator" mentioned by Smokey is what is referred to by some as a "ventilator" in older engines. The 283 V8 in my parents ‘67 Chev has one. It literally is supposed to "separate" the oil vapour from the crankcase air before the gases are sucked through the rest of the PCV system to be burnt. I believe that baffles in the valve covers of more modern engines are supposed to accomplish the same thing.<p><br>
Z||000012||theogre||08-25-99||10:08 PM||||the baffles, in the valve cover, generally only keep the opening from being sprayed by the rockers.<p>the "seperator" does a bit better job. it's usually metal, and cooler than the block, so it dumps a good bit of heat if it's done right. I've run into them on a few things over the years. kind of unusual these days to see one.
Z||000013||Patrick||08-26-99||12:05 AM||||<br>From Ogre's description, this "separator" device sounds completely different than the "ventilator" that the ol' ‘67 Chev has. The ventilator is a cylindrical shaped device that sits horizontally in the lifter valley below the intake manifold towards the back of the block.<p>By no means was I insinuating that they do as good of a job as the other, older methods, but the baffles around the underside of the PCV and breather valve cover openings are ALL that the newer engines have.<p><p>
